<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Portable Computer-Use policy + crypto core for the OpenBurnBar Windows port
    (Phase 4 · W5). This is the PLATFORM-INDEPENDENT half of the Computer-Use
    subsystem: the capability-token issuer/verifier (Ed25519, attestation-bound,
    single-use nonce), the tamper-evident audit hash-chain + Ed25519 signed head,
    the triple-independent kill-switch state machine (hotkey + workspace-lock +
    Remote-Config, fail-closed-on-RC-error), the deny-region / scope matcher, the
    capability-gate decision tree, and the watchdog command/response wire
    protocol.

    It has NO Windows / WinUI / P/Invoke dependency on purpose — the SAME managed
    assembly is:
      * consumed by the WinUI app and the Windows adapter project
        (OpenBurnBar.ComputerUse.Windows, net8.0-windows) whose SendInput / UIA /
        Windows.Graphics.Capture / named-pipe adapters implement the interfaces in
        Adapters/, AND
      * unit-tested on the macOS authoring host today via `dotnet test`
        (windows/tests/computeruse), exactly the pattern already proven by the
        storage seam and the Pal.Ipc signed-nonce handshake.

    Parity sources (macOS, OpenBurnBarCore/Sources/OpenBurnBarComputerUseCore/):
      * CapabilityToken.swift / CapabilityTokenSigning.swift / CapabilityTokenIssuer.swift
        / CapabilityTokenVerifier.swift / CapabilityTokenIssuerTrustMaterial.swift
                                              -> Capability/*.cs
      * ComputerUseAuditChain.swift / ComputerUseAuditLogger.swift /
        ComputerUseAuditSignedHead.swift / ComputerUseAuditHeadFinalizer.swift /
        ComputerUseAuditVerifier.swift        -> Audit/*.cs
      * ComputerUseScopeRule.swift / ComputerUseDenyRegistry.swift -> Scope/*.cs
      * ComputerUseCapabilityGate.swift / ComputerUseBudgetEnvelope.swift /
        ComputerUseActionDescriptor.swift / ComputerUseSessionMetadata.swift -> Gate/*.cs
      * PrivilegedInputKillSwitch.swift + ComputerUsePanicHaltCoordinator.swift (Mac glue)
                                              -> KillSwitch/*.cs
      * PrivilegedInputKillSwitchWatchdogMain.swift (daemon) -> Watchdog/*.cs
      * AppCheckAttestationBinding.swift      -> AttestationBinding.cs

    Ed25519 (RFC 8032) is provided by the pure-managed, cross-platform
    BouncyCastle.Cryptography package so the token/audit signatures are REAL
    crypto on both the macOS authoring host and Windows CI (the .NET BCL has no
    built-in Ed25519). SHA-256 uses the BCL. The crypto lives behind the
    ICapabilitySigner / ICapabilityVerifier seams so a Windows dev host can swap
    in a CNG/TPM-backed signer without touching the state machines.

    Targets net8.0 so the net8.0-windows adapter + WinUI app can reference it,
    while the net10.0 test project consumes it too (a lower TFM is usable by a
    higher one).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <RootNamespace>OpenBurnBar.ComputerUse.Core</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>OpenBurnBar.ComputerUse.Core</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenBurnBar.ComputerUse.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
